/*****************************************************************************
    containerT -- LONGEST-ENTRY MATCH in testContainer. 2026-08-02.

    THE DEFECT (Tony's finding): testContainer built its token with a greedy
    scan over the container's CHARACTER SET, then looked the whole thing up
    once. Character-set membership answers "could this character belong to some
    entry" -- it can never answer "is this prefix an entry", because a set has
    no notion of where an entry ends. So any container holding both a symbol
    and a word poisons the symbol with the word's letters.

    Operators is such a container: `negate` and `modedOP` are word-spelled, so
    n e g a t m o d are all in its set. `9 -grup` therefore scanned `-g`, which
    is an entry of nothing, and the whole match failed -- taking the enclosing
    statement's parse with it, silently, at exit 0.

    THE FIX: the greedy scan is an UPPER BOUND. Back the buffer off one
    character at a time until it IS an entry or is empty. Longest wins.

    ⚠ WHY THE ROWS LOOK LIKE THIS -- the discriminator is a LETTER, not an
    operator. Every row is a matched PAIR: the same operator applied to `grup`
    (whose leading g IS in the Operators set) and to `brup` (whose leading b is
    NOT). Before the fix the two halves disagreed; after it they agree. A row
    that only used one name would be silent, not green.

    MEASURED, both directions, on the pre-fix and post-fix binaries:
        row   pre-fix   post-fix
        A     0         5          --grup   (refused -> parses)
        B     5         5          --brup   (control: never refused)
        C     0         4          9 -grup  (refused -> correct answer)
        D     4         4          9 -brup  (control)
        E     0         9          zed ==grup (refused -> `==` beats `=`)
    A's value is NOT pinned at 4: `--` on a non-iterator is Tony's open
    iterator semantics. What IS pinned is A == B -- the g/b asymmetry is what
    this fixture is about, and it is gone.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT (standing harness rule H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

register(ContainerT);
define
    grup;
    brup;
    zed;

    /*  the presenting bug and its control: a two-character entry whose
        following character is / is not in the container's set               */
    decGrup  code={ grup = 5; --grup; return grup; };
    decBrup  code={ brup = 5; --brup; return brup; };

    /*  a ONE-character entry, which is where a set-poisoned scan hurts most  */
    subGrup  code={ grup = 5; zed = 9; zed = zed -grup; return zed; };
    subBrup  code={ brup = 5; zed = 9; zed = zed -brup; return zed; };

    /*  LONGEST WINS, from a real container: `=` and `==` are both Operators
        entries and `==g` backs off to `==`, never to `=`. If it backed off to
        `=` this would be an ASSIGNMENT and zed would come back 5.            */
    eqGrup   code={ grup = 5; zed = 9; zed ==grup; return zed; };
    eqBrup   code={ brup = 5; zed = 9; zed ==brup; return zed; };

    /*  THE BACK-OFF ITERATES MORE THAN ONCE: `&&g` is 3 characters, `&&` is
        not an entry, `&` is. So this only parses if the loop runs 3 -> 2 -> 1
        rather than trying a single shorter length.                           */
    ampGrup  code={ grup = 5; zed = 9; zed = zed &&grup; return zed; };

    /*  NO ENTRY AT ANY LENGTH: `~` appears only inside `~=`, so `~~g` backs
        off through 3, 2, 1 and empties. The contract is a CLEAN false with the
        mark unmoved -- observable here as a contained refusal: this action is
        rejected, the run does not crash or hang, and every row after it is
        unaffected. (What is asserted is containment, not the mark itself.)    */
    tilGrup  code={ grup = 5; zed = 9; zed = zed ~~grup; return zed; };
    ;

print "A --grup       ->[" decGrup() "]   (pin: A == B, not a literal value)":;
print "B --brup       ->[" decBrup() "]   (the control)":;
print "C 9 -grup      ->[" subGrup() "]   want 4":;
print "D 9 -brup      ->[" subBrup() "]   want 4":;
print "E zed ==grup   ->[" eqGrup() "]   want 9  (5 means `=` won: shortest, not longest)":;
print "F zed ==brup   ->[" eqBrup() "]   want 9   (the control)":;
print "G zed &&grup   ->[" ampGrup() "]   parses only if the back-off iterates":;
print "H zed ~~grup   ->[" tilGrup() "]   refused on purpose -- no entry at any length":;

print "CT SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
